{
  "statement": "Performance efficiency is essential for the library management system to ensure optimal user experience. Quick search response times (EFF-01) enhance usability, while allowing up to 10 simultaneous borrow operations (EFF-02) maximizes resource utilization. Real-time tracking of book availability (EFF-03) provides accurate information, and processing returns within 1 second (EFF-04) maintains high throughput. Timely overdue notifications (EFF-05) hold members accountable, and enabling librarians to add/remove books swiftly (EFF-06) ensures efficient inventory management. Maintaining a 99.5% system uptime (EFF-07) guarantees consistent availability for users.",
  "conflicts_raised": [
    {
      "ids": ["SAF-01", "EFF-02"],
      "type": "resource",
      "description": "EFF-02's allowance of 10 simultaneous borrow operations conflicts with SAF-01's limit of 5 books per member, potentially compromising performance efficiency."
    },
    {
      "ids": ["SAF-02", "EFF-05"],
      "type": "redundant",
      "description": "Both SAF-02 and EFF-05 propose notifying members of overdue books, leading to redundancy in the notification system."
    },
    {
      "ids": ["GRN-03", "EFF-05"],
      "type": "resource",
      "description": "GRN-03's digital notifications for overdue books may conflict with EFF-05's requirement for timely notifications if not implemented efficiently."
    }
  ],
